# SQLite local (or postgres:// via pgx, implemented internal/storage/postgres.go:7)
DATABASE_URL=sqlite:mathua.db
# Example: DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@localhost/mathua?sslmode=disable
PORT=8080
# REQUIRED in production — if unset, a random HS256 secret is generated each run and all tokens invalidate on restart
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Zero-config alternative (SQLite only): leave unset and the server persists an
# auto-generated secret to <db-dir>/.jwt_secret (0600) on first boot.
# JWT_SECRET_FILE can point at an explicit path instead. Postgres deployments
# have no shared file — set JWT_SECRET there.
JWT_SECRET=
#JWT_SECRET_FILE=/data/.jwt_secret
# Split-dev frontend origin (e.g. http://localhost:3000): Google OAuth lands
# the token here. Unset = relative redirect (single-binary deployments).
#FRONTEND_URL=https://mathua.com
# Password-reset mail (optional): unset SMTP_HOST disables emailed resets
# (endpoint 503s honestly); logged-in password change always works.
#SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
#SMTP_PORT=587
#SMTP_USER=bot@example.com
#SMTP_PASS=secret
#SMTP_FROM=bot@example.com
# CORS allowlist — comma-separated; empty = allow all (dev). In production MUST include your frontend origin.
# Wildcard "*" suffix supported, e.g. "https://*.preview.example.com" for preview deployments.
# Example: CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://mathua.com,https://www.mathua.com,http://localhost:3000
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,https://mathua.com
# Administration (Admin V2). There is no password to configure here. Administrative access is a
# role on an account — owner, admin or moderator — read from the database on every request, and
# the first owner is granted from the machine with:
#
#   mathua admin promote <email>
#
# Everyone else is added from /admin/contributors by an owner or admin, through an invitation
# that is bound to an email and stored only as a hash. Nothing about administration needs a
# deployment secret, which is the point: a shared password cannot say which person used it.
# Google OAuth (optional) — enables One-Tap + redirect login. Keep username/password intact.
# Create at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials → OAuth 2.0 Client ID (Web)
# Authorized origins: https://mathua.com,http://localhost:3000
# Authorized redirect: https://mathua.com/api/auth/google/callback
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URL=https://mathua.com/api/auth/google/callback
# Other OAuth providers (optional, each enables its login button + connect):
# GitHub: https://github.com/settings/developers → OAuth App, callback
#   <origin>/api/auth/github/callback (needs read:user user:email).
#GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
#GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Facebook: https://developers.facebook.com/apps → Meta Login, callback
#   <origin>/api/auth/facebook/callback (email needs app review).
#FACEBOOK_APP_ID=
#FACEBOOK_APP_SECRET=
# Microsoft (personal + work/school): https://portal.azure.com → App
#   registration, redirect <origin>/api/auth/microsoft/callback.
#MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
#MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Apple: Services ID + key (ES256, 6-month rotation); redirect
#   <origin>/api/auth/apple/callback. Never matches private-relay emails.
#APPLE_CLIENT_ID=
#APPLE_TEAM_ID=
#APPLE_KEY_ID=
#APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Optional AI augmentation. Off unless AI_ENABLED is set. Nothing here is on the
# learning path: grading, scheduling, mastery and the onboarding fallback are
# deterministic and unchanged when AI is off or a provider fails.
#
# Privacy rules the code enforces, not this file:
#   - A request is only sent to a provider whose declared policy permits its data
#     class. Identifiable learner data is denied by default.
#   - A graded expected answer is never a request field and is never forwarded.
#   - Unknown, unverified providers are ineligible; there is no fallback that
#     widens the data class.
AI_ENABLED=false
# "production" marks real learner traffic. Dev-only providers are inert here even
# if AI_DEV_PROVIDERS=1, so a flag left set cannot route a learner to a trial
# endpoint.
AI_ENV=development
# Enables the dev/eval-only providers below (NVIDIA NIM, Cerebras, OpenRouter,
# OpenCode Zen). They are never eligible in production.
AI_DEV_PROVIDERS=false
# Preferred provider order, by id. Unlisted providers keep their default order.
# Example: AI_PROVIDER_CHAIN=groq,cloudflare
AI_PROVIDER_CHAIN=
# Per-identity daily request cap. Identities are a student id when authenticated
# and an IP otherwise, because guest ids are freely mintable. The engine-level
# free tiers are shared by the whole app, so keep this small.
AI_DAILY_USER_CAP=5
# Application-wide daily request cap, across every identity. The per-identity cap
# bounds one learner; this bounds the whole app, which is what protects a shared
# free tier from many learners at once. Both are reserved in one transaction, so
# a request refused by either consumes neither. 0 disables the ceiling.
AI_GLOBAL_DAILY_CAP=800
# Per-request token budgets. A reasoning model spends part of every budget on
# hidden reasoning tokens before it emits an answer, so these are generous enough
# that the budget is not the thing that fails. A completion that runs out is an
# error, never a truncated reply.
AI_ASSISTANT_MAX_TOKENS=600
AI_GOAL_MAX_TOKENS=600
# Zero-data retention confirmation. Set to 1 only after Zero Data Retention is
# actually switched on in the provider's console/account. In production
# (AI_ENV=production) a provider that declares zero retention is refused until
# this is set, because "the switch exists" is not "the switch is on". AI simply
# stays off — the rest of the app is unaffected.
AI_ZDR_CONFIRMED=
# The header a trusted edge overwrites with the real client IP, used to key rate
# limits and guest AI budgets. Leave unset on Fly (FLY_APP_NAME alone enables
# Fly-Client-IP) and on a directly exposed process. Set it for another proxy,
# e.g. AI_TRUSTED_IP_HEADER=CF-Connecting-IP behind Cloudflare. Trusting a header
# a client can set is a rate-limit and quota bypass.
AI_TRUSTED_IP_HEADER=

# Production chain: Groq head, Cloudflare Workers AI failover.
# Groq. Free tier limits apply at the organisation level — one key shares the
# whole app's budget, which is why the per-user cap and the failover exist.
# Enable Zero Data Retention in the Groq console (Data Controls) before treating
# prompts as not retained.
GROQ_API_KEY=
#GROQ_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
#GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b
# Reasoning budget for the Groq model. "low" keeps the hidden-reasoning spend
# small; without it a 200-token request spent 135 tokens on reasoning and failed.
#GROQ_REASONING_EFFORT=low
# Cloudflare Workers AI. Both the account id and the token are required; the
# base path is built from the account id.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
#CLOUDFLARE_MODEL=@cf/meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct

# Dev/eval-only providers. Each is inert unless AI_DEV_PROVIDERS=true AND
# AI_ENV is not production. They receive public/synthetic content only, and a dev
# provider with no model id is skipped rather than defaulted, because Mathua has
# not verified their model ids.
#NVIDIA_API_KEY=
#NVIDIA_MODEL=
#CEREBRAS_API_KEY=
#CEREBRAS_MODEL=
#OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
#OPENROUTER_MODEL=
#OPENCODE_API_KEY=
#OPENCODE_MODEL=

